WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:06.600
From major franchises to brutally clever indie
hits, 2017 was a fantastic year for horror.

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:11.040
Sure, you probably already know about heavy
hitters like It and Get Out, but plenty of

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:13.400
other great horror films were also released
during the year.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:17.520
If you're in the mood for some spine-tingling
additions to your queue, here are our picks

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.600
for the best horror movies of 2017.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:20.820
Split

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.180
M. Night Shyamalan has had a rocky decade
or so, but Split was definitely a return to

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.040
his twisty form.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:31.860
The story focused on the abduction of a group
of young women by a man with multiple personalities,

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:37.420
which made for a taut and claustrophobic thriller,
anchored by actor James McAvoy's transitions

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.280
between his character's many identities.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:44.420
Critics praised his performance, as well as
Shyamalan's ability to balance those shifting

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:48.670
points of view—and channel the filmmaking
prowess that made him a name to watch after

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:49.460
The Sixth Sense.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:50.580
Even better?

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.129
The director used his trademark twist in a
way that no one saw coming, setting up a must-see

00:00:56.129 --> 00:00:56.629
sequel.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.160
Stephen King's It

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.040
Send in the clowns.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:06.140
Stephen King's It was previously adapted as
a TV miniseries in 1990, and that live-action

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.660
version of the author's horror classic still
gives us nightmares—but it's nothing compared

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.180
to director Andy Muschietti's big-screen version.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.200
"Hiya Georgie.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:19.560
What a nice boat."

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.940
The film earned rave reviews, with critics
hailing it as a terrifying and a clever exploration

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:29.280
of the psychological trauma King captured
so well in his novel.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.780
Bill Skarsgård's take on Pennywise the Clown
is basically face-painted nightmare fuel,

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:38.400
so taking a trip to the sewers of Derry is
well worth the risk.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.740
Because hey, we all float down there, right?

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:42.220
Get Out

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.740
This isn't just one of the best horror movies
of 2017—it's one of the best horror films

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:48.720
of the past decade.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.920
Writer and director Jordan Peele leaves you
guessing right up until the end, slowly ratcheting

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.420
up the terror with a story that starts out
like any other innocent, average dramedy.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.460
Peel back a few layers, though, and the story
tackles race relations through the lens of

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:09.110
a horror tale, serving up a sharp, thoughtful,
funny blend of horror and social satire that

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:14.400
treads almost a bit too close to reality at
times in ways that will make your skin crawl.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:16.140
The Devil's Candy

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.260
This indie horror flick focuses on an artist
who starts painting terrifying images after

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:25.800
moving into a new home—and then someone
starts terrorizing him and his family.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.640
"I don't remember painting this."

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.940
This scary look into the depths of human depravity
doubles as a clever subversion of the horror

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:38.720
movie tropes viewers have come to expect and
was thus one of the most underrated movies

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:39.240
of the year.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:40.780
Lake Bodom

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:45.940
If you're looking for a throwback to the classic
days when machete-wielding maniacs prowled

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:51.420
the woods and menaced unsuspecting teens,
book a trip to director Taneli Mustonen's

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:52.100
Lake Bodom.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:57.700
This classically styled slasher focuses on
the urban legend of a viciously fatal night

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:02.580
that occurred decades ago, as a group of teens
plan a trip to investigate and recreate the

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:03.760
events of the old tale.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.080
But it turns out they're in for a lot more
than a mere homage.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.600
Even if the movie doesn't exactly reinvent
the gore genre, this is still a scary good

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:12.100
time.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:13.340
Life

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:18.820
This space-set sci-fi horror flick hit the
sweet spot by throwing The Thing, Gravity

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.460
and Alien into a blender.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.940
The result was a whole lot of terrifying fun.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:28.040
It focuses on the crew of the International
Space Station as they investigate a mysterious

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.100
new life form that's been recovered on Mars.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:35.060
Their experiments quickly take a turn for
the worse when they realize this alien creature

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:39.520
might be a lot smarter -- and more sinister
-- than they'd given it credit for.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.700
"Life's very existence requires destruction."

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:44.480
It Comes at Night

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:50.320
This post-apocalyptic, psychological horror
flick picks up after a virus has wiped out

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.000
most of the world's population.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:56.640
The story focuses on a family that's cut itself
off from the rest of the world to avoid the

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:57.140
plague.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:02.080
When another group of survivors join them,
paranoia and fear of what might be in the

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.140
woods drives them beyond the edge.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.580
Critics praised the film for finding terror
in the relationship among survivors, and bringing

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:13.000
a refreshingly simple doom to a tale of the
apocalypse.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:14.940
A Dark Song

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:19.400
When you dabble with the dark arts, it's almost
certainly destined to go wrong.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:24.580
That's the premise behind A Dark Song, which
follows two people who retreat into a house

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:28.180
in the woods to try and make contact with
those in the beyond.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.160
What happens will stick with you long after
the movie ends, nestling into your psyche

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:34.520
for some lingering scares.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.460
It might've initially flown under the radar,
but A Dark Song is well worth a visit.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:40.680
Raw

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.100
The story of a vegetarian who develops a taste
for raw meat is an interesting narrative,

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:51.900
but Raw wrings that premise for all it's worth
and more, telling a story with deep social

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:56.330
and emotional poignancy, with a clever spin
on the challenges of adolescence.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:02.760
And the way writer and director Julia Ducournau
wraps that story is a gory masterpiece positively

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.640
dripping with viscera and blood.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:06.420
The Girl With All the Gifts

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:11.660
One of the smartest zombie movies in recent
years, The Girl With All the Gifts uses the

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:15.680
undead to explore some deeper emotional and
philosophical questions.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:20.820
The story focuses on a group of children who
are somehow immune to an epidemic disease

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.550
which has destroyed the world, and the role
they can play in saving humanity.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:30.080
It might be a post-apocalyptic zombie story
with doses of sci-fi, but the film is packed

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:31.660
with more than a few good scares.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:33.500
Annabelle: Creation

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:38.200
The Conjuring has quickly become one of the
biggest horror franchises of the decade, and

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:41.460
the latest spinoff from the creepy mothership
does not disappoint.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:46.740
Annabelle: Creation, the second film to focus
on the nefarious antique doll featured in

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:50.920
the main Conjuring films, jumps back in time
to tell an early story from when Annabelle

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.800
was still a relatively new danger-doll.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:57.670
A marked improvement over the first Annabelle
film, the film was a surprisingly moody and

00:05:57.670 --> 00:05:58.780
effective horror yarn.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:00.620
​Happy Death Day

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:05.620
If you're looking for a horror flick with
some biting humor, make a date with Happy

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:09.600
Death Day—it's Groundhog Day with a bloody
slasher twist.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:14.639
The film centers on a college student who's
executed on her birthday, which she has to

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.320
keep reliving over and over until she can
figure out who attacked her.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:23.380
The story has charm and, although derivative,
offers up some authentic thrills and humor

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:25.660
for audiences of the jump-scare persuasion.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:27.280
The Babysitter

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:32.860
Netflix's The Babysitter is one heck of a
fun little horror romp, best described as

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:38.080
an R-rated, blood-soaked, titillating modern-day
spin on a 1980s horror B-movie.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:42.640
It's obviously low-budget, but although it's
not high art, the film is still well worth

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:44.920
the hour-and-a-half it takes to kick back
and enjoy it.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:49.780
It's a cheesy good time, so give it a shot
and The Babysitter might just be the silly

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:51.460
little surprise you needed this year.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:53.220
Better Watch Out

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:58.580
Critics are calling this one "Home Alone meets
The Strangers" because the film puts a spin

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:00.660
on the home invasion thriller formula.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:05.260
Better Watch Out follows what happens when
invaders break in to a home protected by a

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:06.800
group of kids and their babysitter.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:12.020
Instead of retreating in fear, the kids start
setting booby traps and have a whole lot of

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:16.919
R-rated fun in the process to protect the
house -- with plenty of twists to unwrap before

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:17.680
the credits roll.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:19.960
"Isn't this exciting?"

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:21.700
Super Dark Times

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:28.200
If you're looking for a VHS-era throwback,
the 1990s-set Super Dark Times finds terror

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.300
in the simple, boring life in suburbia.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:36.240
The visceral thriller is considered "a coming-of-age
nightmare" and landed to near universal critical

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:36.740
acclaim.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.900
The story follows a group of teens who are
driven apart following the disappearance of

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:45.960
another teen, and presents a bygone era of
innocence through a bloody intense lens.

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:47.740
Gerald's Game

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:52.450
Stephen King's collection has been all over
the small and silver screens this year, but

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:56.919
the latest adaptation quietly slipped under
the radar—despite being one of the best

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:57.420
of the bunch.

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:02.640
Gerald's Game follows a wife who's left shackled
to a bed after a weekend getaway with her

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.060
husband takes a dark turn.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:09.020
She's forced to face everything from starvation
to her own doubts and demons as she tries

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:11.160
to find a way to escape and survive.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:17.480
The novel was previously thought to be unfilmable,
so imagine everyone's surprise when the movie

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:22.180
not only happened -- but turned out to be
pretty great, without sacrificing the meat

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:22.900
of the story.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:24.200
Thanks for watching!

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.900
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.320
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

